**Cycle 15 (research lane, deepening) ran and landed.** I picked the one item both lanes were blocked on: the pre-registered cell "why does the 10-year-yield tailwind effect live only in 2024-25", testing whether a rate-driven regime, measured by the 60-day rolling correlation between gold daily returns and daily yield changes, conditions the surviving Δ80 tailwind cell. The analysis ran synchronously in 79 seconds on existing caches only (no scan, no MT5, no external calls).

**Verdict: unidentifiable in the out-of-sample window, null wherever identifiable.**

| Window | Share of bars with negative correlation | δ = β_neg − β_non (cluster t) | Balanced-split δ |
|---|---|---|---|
| In-sample 2010-20 | 0.86 | −0.029 (−0.62) | −0.028 (−0.71) |
| Out-of-sample 2021-26 | 0.91 (over the 0.90 cap, rule ⑬) | −0.086 (−0.60) | −0.053 (−0.78) |
| Recent 2024-26 | 0.83 | −0.018 (−0.13) | +0.018 (+0.23) |

**The structural fact that drives the result.** Gold's daily correlation with nominal yields was negative on 97 to 100 percent of days every year from 2014 through 2024. So "rate-driven period" cannot separate 2021-22, where the effect was zero, from 2024-25, where it peaked. The correlation then weakens toward zero in 2025 and 2026H1, exactly when the effect is strongest. The pre-written reading (ii) applies: the 2024-25 localization is not explained by gold reacting to rates at the daily scale, which also weakens the "rates drive gold drift" mechanism that cycle 13 already found only half-funded.

**Strongest counter-argument recorded.** The meter is daily co-movement, while the effect lives at an 80-day trend scale. Slow coupling between gold's trend and the rate trend could exist even when daily correlation is near zero, so the null may be a scale mismatch. One descriptive residual was logged for forward data only: in both windows the quintile with the weakest correlation had the largest effect, but that is the maximum of 15 post-hoc cells and does not survive correction.

